  <h2>What Mobile Testing Covers Beyond Just Tapping Through the App</h2>
  <p>Mobile testing is often reduced to functional testing on a simulator – verifying that buttons do what they're supposed to do. That is necessary but not sufficient. Mobile apps fail in production for reasons that have nothing to do with button behavior.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Device compatibility:</strong> Does the app render correctly on small screens, large screens, notched displays, and foldable form factors? Different manufacturers customize Android in ways that affect layout behavior even for the same OS version.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation and upgrade paths:</strong> Does a fresh install work correctly? Does upgrading from the previous version preserve user data and settings? Does downgrading to a previous version cause data corruption?</li>
    <li><strong>Permission handling:</strong> When users deny location, camera, or notification permissions, does the app handle it gracefully? When permissions are revoked while the app is running, does it crash or degrade cleanly?</li>
    <li><strong>Offline and poor network behavior:</strong> What happens when the network drops mid-operation? Does the app show meaningful error states? Does it retry correctly when connectivity is restored? Does it corrupt local data if a sync is interrupted?</li>
    <li><strong>Battery and memory conditions:</strong> Does the app behave correctly when the device is at 5% battery and in battery saver mode? Does it leak memory during extended use?</li>
    <li><strong>Background and foreground transitions:</strong> When the app is backgrounded and foregrounded, is state preserved correctly? Do in-progress operations (form submissions, media playback) resume correctly?</li>
  </ul>

