    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">Do you test on real devices?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">Yes. We use BrowserStack for broad device coverage and maintain a small pool of physical devices for testing scenarios that emulators don't handle well – biometric authentication, camera functionality, push notification behavior, and Bluetooth interactions.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How do you handle Android fragmentation?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">We prioritize Android versions based on your analytics data – typically the last three major versions – and test across the screen sizes and manufacturers your users actually have. We use BrowserStack's device matrix to cover the most common device/OS combinations without needing a physical lab.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">Can you test React Native apps?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">Yes. We test the shared JavaScript business logic via Jest and platform-specific UI and behaviors via Detox for iOS and Appium for Android. Platform-specific behaviors get dedicated test cases for iOS and Android separately.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What is mobile performance testing?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">Mobile performance testing checks how the app behaves under resource constraints: startup time, memory usage during extended use, CPU behavior during background tasks, and battery consumption for long-running operations. These are especially important on mid-range Android devices.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">Do you test app store submission requirements?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">We review apps against the most common App Store and Play Store rejection criteria: privacy policy requirements, required permissions justifications, content rating accuracy, and metadata accuracy. We can catch the common rejection reasons before submission.</div>
    </div>
